  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThe forecast is again working out fairly well this morning as some \njet enhanced precipitation is currently working its way across \nroughly the southern half of the area, including here at the humble \nRiverton Weather Abode. It is rain at our office right now, but \nthere has been some snow in places like Pinedale and Big Piney. \nA few lightning strikes have also been noted. Almost all showers \nshould end by sunrise today. However, there will likely be some \nslick spots on roads that saw snow. And, a few locations have seen \nthe fog monster crawl out of this lair to feed on the moisture from \nthe precipitation.\n\nOnce the showers end, ridging should build across the area and bring \nat least two days of fairly tranquil weather with many areas having \nthree days of fairly quiet weather, lasting through Friday. There \nwill still be somewhat of a pressure gradient in the wind prone \nareas from  Muddy Gap through Casper so a gusty wind will continue \nthere, but not nearly as strong as yesterday. Wind should decrease \nthrough Friday as well. Temperatures will also be on the rise. The \ntemperatures in the forecast are probably the coolest it will be. I \ncould see it 3 to 5 degrees higher on each day. The warmest day \nlooks to be Friday when places like Casper may be well up into the \n60s. \n\nThe next chance of showers will begin to move into western Wyoming \nas a trough rotates around an upper level low off of the Pacific \nNorthwest Coast and brings increasing moisture to the area. There \ncontinues to be disagreement in the guidance in regards to placement \nand intensity of the showers, with some models, like the GFS, \nkeeping things mainly dry through most of Saturday. For now, most \nareas have some chance of Saturday but most places should be dry \nmost of the time.\n\nThe most interesting feature of the forecast though is from Sunday \ninto Monday. There is an interesting and complex situation setting \nup. We have two main players. The first is the aforementioned upper \nlevel low that will get kicked eastward into the Rockies Sunday \nnight and into Monday. Another player is an area of low pressure \nfrom further south in the Pacific Ocean that will move into southern \nCalifornia on Sunday and track east to northeastward into the \nsouthern Rockies. The big question is how will these systems \ninteract, how much moisture from the much wetter southern system can \nget drawn northward and if the systems phase, where will they phase. \nThe guidance, of both the deterministic and ensemble varieties, \ncontinues to be have a rather wide spread in regards to all of these \nvariables. In the deterministic realm, the European phases the \nsystems further eastward and is slower, which would mean a wetter \nsolution. The GFS is faster and drier, with the phasing and \ndeepening of the low further east. So, although we are fairly \nconfident of at least some unsettled weather across the area, \nprecipitation amounts and the placement and timing of precipitation \nremains highly uncertain. A first look at the NBM ensemble gives at \nleast a 1 in 2 chance for most areas to get at least a quarter of an \ninch or QPF from Sunday morning through Monday evening. As for \nsnow, the 1 in2  chance of 6 inches over the same time frame is \nlargely confined to the mountains above 8000 feet. It could get cold \nenough for lower elevations to see the first snowflakes of the year, \nbut chances of decent accumulations of greater than 2 inches remains \nlow, under 1 out of 4 at this time. But remember, this is still 5 \ndays out so a lot can and probably will change.Stay tuned as we get \ncloser to the event for more clarity. After this system departs, \nthere is good model agreement in ridging building over the area, \npossibly bringing dry and tranquil for much of next week.",
